ontaining tiles of all three suits, wind tiles and dragon tiles.\" It doesn't specify they must be in melds; just present. So even if I have a pair of wind, it's still wind tiles. So discarding F1 might keep All Types if I still have at least one wind tile. But I would lose the Prevalent Wind points. However, if I discard one F1, I have two F1s, which could be a pair. Then my hand would be: melds: W3 kong, T2-T3-T4 chow, B7 pung, and then hand: T2, F1, F1, J3. That's four tiles. I need one meld and a pair? Actually, with two F1s as pair, then T2 and J3 are lone tiles. So I need a meld from T2 and J3? Not possible. So I still need to draw a tile to form a meld with T2 or J3, or to pair with one of them. So discarding F1 doesn't improve readiness.\n\nGiven that, keeping the F1 pung gives me a ready meld, so I only need a pair. So I should keep the F1 pung and discard either T2 or J3. Between T2 and J3, which is more valuable? J3 gives dragon for All Types. Discarding J3 loses dragon, so I would need to draw a dragon later to get All Types. But there are only 4 J3 in total. Unshown tiles: J3: 1 remaining (since I have one in hand, and unshown shows J3:1, so total one left in wall? Actually, unshown tiles list shows counts of tiles not visible to anyone? It says \"Unshown tiles are: ... J3: 1\". That means there is one J3 tile not yet seen (in wall or dead). I have one J3 in hand, so total J3 tiles: 4 copies. Others: from histories, I see J3 played: my history: Play J3, so I discarded one earlier? Actually, my history: \"Play J3\" is listed, so I discarded a J3 before. Also, next player history: \"Play J3\". So at least two J3 have been discarded. Plus I have one now. So possibly three J3 are out. Unshown says 1, so maybe only one left. So drawing another J3 is low probability. So if I discard J3, I might not get another dragon easily. However, for All Types, I need a dragon tile. If I discard J3, I must draw a different dragon (J1 or J2) to have a dragon. Unshown: J1:0